>
  <p>Condensation is easy to dismiss when it appears as a little water on a bedroom window.</p>
  <p>The concern is what happens repeatedly.</p>
  <p>Moisture can collect on glass, frames, walls and behind furniture each cold morning. Paint may discolour, wardrobes can smell musty and mould may return soon after it has been wiped away.</p>
  <p>Cleaning visible growth may be appropriate in limited circumstances, but it does not explain why the surface remains damp.</p>
  <p>Winter condensation usually involves three conditions:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>moisture inside the home</li>
    <li>a surface cold enough for that moisture to condense</li>
    <li>insufficient ventilation or drying for the surface to recover</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A leak can create similar signs and may occur at the same time, but condensation and water entry are separate causes that require different responses.</p>

  <h2>Our Take</h2>
  <p>Find the water before treating the stain.</p>
  <p>A wet window every morning, mould behind a wardrobe or recurring ceiling discolouration is evidence of a moisture problem, not simply a cleaning problem.</p>
  <p>Ask:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>Where is the moisture coming from?</li>
    <li>Why is this surface becoming cold or staying wet?</li>
    <li>Can the room ventilate and dry?</li>
    <li>Does the pattern suggest water entering through a defect?</li>
    <li>Is the affected material still sound and suitable for household cleaning?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Reduce the moisture source, improve controlled ventilation and address unusually cold surfaces. Escalate extensive, recurring or concealed mould rather than repeatedly applying stronger products.</p>

  <h2>How winter condensation forms</h2>
  <p>Indoor air contains water vapour. When moisture-laden air reaches a sufficiently cold surface, some vapour becomes liquid water.</p>
  <p>Common winter surfaces include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>window glass and aluminium frames</li>
    <li>external walls and room corners</li>
    <li>ceilings beneath cold roof spaces</li>
    <li>the backs of wardrobes</li>
    <li>walls behind beds and sofas</li>
    <li>skylights and cold water pipes</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Cold nights lower surface temperatures. Closed windows reduce uncontrolled heat loss but can also reduce drying. People continue to breathe, shower, cook and wash clothes, so indoor moisture can build while the surfaces around it become colder.</p>
  <p>The temperature at which condensation begins changes with air temperature and moisture content. A humidity reading can provide useful context, but no single number or appliance setting explains every room.</p>
  <p>Visible droplets are surface condensation. Moisture may also occur within a wall, roof, ceiling or floor assembly. Concealed moisture can eventually appear as staining, softened plasterboard, damaged insulation, corrosion, timber decay or a musty smell. Do not open a wall or ceiling simply to investigate it; concealed problems require building-specific assessment.</p>

  <h2>Condensation or a leak?</h2>
  <p>Do not automatically label every damp patch as condensation, and do not assume every wet window is leaking.</p>
  <p class="table-hint">Swipe sideways to compare the full table.</p>
